    "text": "Preparing Ethers"
  },
  "documentClass": null,
  "metadata": {
    "uuid": "99f1de15-b612-4c57-9e34-bba3194c7207"
  },
  "content": [
    {
      "type": "para",
      "id": "para-00001",
      "segmentId": "m00249:para:para-00001"
    },
    {
      "type": "figure",
      "id": "fig-00001",
      "class": "unnumbered",
      "caption": null,
      "media": {
        "alt": {
          "segmentId": "m00249:alt:fig-00001-alt",
          "text": "Reaction mechanism for ethanol to diethyl ether in hydronium involving: protonation of oxygen; ethanol attack, loss of water via S N 2; deprotonation of oxygen to produce neutral product."
        },
        "src": "../../media/OChem_18_02_001.jpg",
        "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
      }
    },
    {
      "type": "section",
      "id": "sect-00001",
      "class": null,
      "title": {
        "segmentId": "m00249:title:sect-00001-title",
        "text": "The Williamson Ether Synthesis"
      },
      "content": [
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00002",
          "segmentId": "m00249:para:para-00002"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00002",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00249:alt:fig-00002-alt",
              "text": "Cyclopentanol reacts with sodium hydride in T H F to form cyclopentanoxide. This attacks methyl iodide to form cyclopentyl methyl ether product (74%)."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_18_02_002.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00003",
          "segmentId": "m00249:para:para-00003"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00003",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00249:alt:fig-00003-alt",
              "text": "Alpha-D-glucose reacts with methyl iodide in presence of silver oxide to form alpha-D-glucose pentamethyl ether (85%) and silver iodide. In product, all hydroxyl hydrogens replace by methyl groups."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_18_02_003.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        },
        {
          "type": "para",
          "id": "para-00004",
          "segmentId": "m00249:para:para-00004"
        },
        {
          "type": "figure",
          "id": "fig-00004",
          "class": "unnumbered scaled-down",
          "caption": null,
          "media": {
            "alt": {
              "segmentId": "m00249:alt:fig-00004-alt",
              "text": "The first reaction generates tert-butyl methyl ether from tert-butoxide and iodomethane. The second reaction shows 2-chloro-2-methylpropane reacts with methoxy ion to form 2-methylpropene, methanol, and chloride ion."
            },
            "src": "../../media/OChem_18_02_004.jpg",
            "mimeType": "image/jpeg"
          }
        }
      ]
    },
